---
// The privacy policy. Written from what the software actually does, and checked against the
// code: what a self-hosted instance sends us (a version string, four counts and its signed
// receipts, only when the owner turns it on), how long the network keeps them, what the hosted
// service would hold, and what the site itself collects.
// It is a draft for a lawyer to review before the hosted service opens, and it says so.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const UPDATED = "22 September 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy · Surfing Dog"
  description="What Surfing Dog knows about you, which for a self-hosted inbox is almost nothing, and what changes when we host it for you."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="privacy-h">What we know about you</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Surfing Dog Inbox runs on your computers, not ours. So for most people reading this, the answer is almost
      nothing, and this page exists to say exactly how much almost nothing is.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}. Surfing Dog Lda, Portugal. Questions: privacy@surfingdog.ai</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="self-h">
    <div class="wrap wrap-read prose">
      <h2 class="h-section" id="self-h">If you run the inbox yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Your customers' messages, bookings and orders are in your database, on your machine. We cannot read them, we
        cannot recover them, and we never receive them. That is not a promise about our intentions; it is a fact about
        where the software runs. The code is open, so you can check it. What can leave, if you switch it on, is set
        out below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things do leave your instance, and only if you switch them on. In Settings, <b>Network</b> is off by
        default. When it is on, your instance registers its domain once and then, every hour, sends its software
        version, which runtime it is on, and how many bookings, orders, quotes and messages it created in the last
        24 hours. Those counts say nothing about anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also sends the network every receipt it issues. A receipt is a signed record, made when a booking is
        confirmed or an order is paid, of which business issued it, its reference for the booking or order, what
        kind of promise it records, and when. The customer in it is a code, not a name: an HMAC made with a key that
        only your instance holds, so the network cannot turn it back into a person, and the same customer at another
        business has a different code. When the booking or order states a price, the receipt carries the amount; a
        paid order's receipt also says how it was paid, such as by card, when your instance knows. If the
        customer's agent counter-signs the receipt, that signature is sent too, with the agent's public key; an agent
        that keeps its key presents the same one at every business, so the receipts it counter-signed can be linked
        to each other. No names, no email addresses, no phone numbers, no postal addresses, no message text, no list
        of what was ordered, no payment references.
      </p>
      <p>
        The network keeps the hourly counts for 90 days, each receipt and its counter-signature for 540 days after
        it arrives, and the totals for each business, how many receipts it issued and how many were counter-signed,
        indefinitely. The directory lists the public profile your manifest already publishes, the version and runtime
        your instance reports and whether it is answering; of the receipts it shows counts and the date of the latest,
        never a receipt, an amount or a customer's code.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn it off and both stop; what the network already holds is kept for the periods above. Point
        <code>network.url</code> at a directory you run instead, and none of it reaches us at all.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">If we host it for you</h2>
      <p>
        Hosted tenancy is not open yet. When it opens, the shape is this. For the data your customers send you, you
        are the controller and we are your processor: we hold it to run the service for you, on our own servers in
        the European Union, and we do not use it for anything else. For your own account, your address, your billing
        and how you use the service, we are the controller.
      </p>
      <p>
        You will be able to export everything as a file and delete everything, from the software, without asking us.
        A written processing agreement will be part of the terms before the first customer is onboarded.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">This website</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics, no advertising, no tracking pixels, no third-party scripts, no cookie banner, because there is
        nothing to consent to. Our servers keep ordinary web logs, including your address and the page you asked for,
        for a short period, to keep the site up and to stop abuse. Cloudflare sits in front of the site and keeps its
        own logs for the same reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you leave your address on the waitlist, or write to us, we keep that message and that address so we can
        answer, and for no other purpose. If you post on the requests board, what you write is public, and the
        address you give is used only to tell you when the thing you asked for ships.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the General Data Protection Regulation you can ask for a copy of what we hold about you, ask us to
        correct it, ask us to delete it, object to what we are doing with it, and complain to a supervisory authority.
        In Portugal that is the <a class="link" href="https://www.cnpd.pt/" rel="noopener">CNPD</a>. Write to
        privacy@surfingdog.ai and we will answer within a month.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where we rely on a legitimate interest rather than your consent, it is to keep the service running and to
        prevent abuse, and you can object to it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">The network and reviews</h2>
      <p>
        The directory and the receipts described above are live. Reputation, for businesses and for customers, is
        being built. Before it collects anything, this page will say exactly what it collects, how long it keeps it
        and who can see it. It will be built only on real transactions, the network itself will decide nothing about
        anyone (each business's own rules will), and identifiers will stay pseudonymous. If any of that changes, the
        change will be on this page before the feature is on.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        This page has a date at the top and a history in public: the site is an open repository, so every edit to this
        text is a commit anyone can read. Material changes will be announced on the blog rather than slipped in.
      </p>

      <div class="glass card" style="margin-top: var(--sp-6)">
        <h3 class="h-card">A note on where this stands</h3>
        <p class="card-text">
          This is an honest description of what the software does today, written by the people who built it and
          checked against the code. It has not yet been reviewed by a lawyer. That review happens before the hosted
          service opens to anyone but us, and this notice comes off the page when it has.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
